> WP4. Perennial Crops & Management
> POLICY OBJECTIVE: Develop the first empirical, global estimates of perennial crop losses under climate change, and how management can avoid these losses.
> METHOD CONTRIBUTIONS: Integrate process-based results into an empirical framework that accounts for hidden variables.
> 
> This work package extends two ground-breaking projects of the PI's, both of which integrate process-based outcomes into an empirical framework to better study climate impacts on agriculture. (1) Rising (2024) provides a framework for studying the hidden role of perennial management (particularly harvesting decisions), which is crucial for understanding their yield outcomes. This approach was extended globally for coffee in Sachs et al (2019), including estimates of the benefits of management (see Figure 3). (2) Unpublished work (Rising & Tahmid WP) directly incorporates three agricultural process-based model outputs into an econometric framework, producing higher performance than either the empirical model or process-based models under cross-validation, and more credibly capturing nonlinearities in biophysical processes and the effects of hidden farmer management.
> 
> This work package would study three cash crops as case studies to address perennials generally: coffee, cocoa, and tea have similar market dynamics and span a range of perennial crop types.
> 
> METHODS (collaborator Muhammad Taky Tahmid, Delaware):
> Construct a global dataset of observed yields, growing areas, process-based outputs and suitability, and agronomically-relevant weather variables: Yield is available at the subnational level for coffee (8 countries, 73% of global production), cocoa (at least 4 countries, 64% of global production), tea (at least 4 countries, 66% of global production), as well as at the national level globally, with most of these sources available for 20 years or more. High resolution maps of the geography of these crops within the regional units will be used to specify weather grid cells to predict each one.
> Integrate the methods in Rising (2024), Sachs et al (2019), and Rising & Tahmid (WP): The econometric approach follows Hultgren et al. (2025), which integrated with process-based outcomes in Rising & Tahmid (WP) and suitability outcomes in Sachs et al (2019). However, perennial crops suffer from lack of data and misreporting of biophysical yields (production divided by planted area), and observed yields (production divided by harvested area) are unreliable and biased (Rising 2024). The solution in Rising (2024) extends econometrics with a Bayesian MCMC to calibrate the model with hidden variables for planting and management.
> Study margins of adaptation: Several margins of adaptation are exposed by the model, and will be studied, and specifically drivers behind model-estimated variation in weather sensitivity, costs of harvest, and replanting rates, across regions; and the role of irrigation and fertilizer use, based on process-based outputs.
