> WP5. Biodiversity & Conservation
> POLICY OBJECTIVES: Identify species at risk of extinction and the characteristics of their biology and nearby human activity associated with high risk. Translate these into improved maps of conservation priorities.
> METHOD CONTRIBUTION: Integrate robust decision-making into a land use optimisation model, to account for the dynamic, uncertain evolution of extinction risk.
> 
> The project relies on a unique dataset: the PI has collected copies of the IUCN Red List database regularly since 2010, planning this analysis for the last 15 years (in 2010, 2013, 2015, 2017, 2020, 2022, and 2025). The Red List reports for each species an endangerment status, population trend, and spatial distribution. Figure 1 shows how plant species have transitioned between risk categories from 2010 and 2025.
> 
> The central modeling assumption is that traditional risk factors, such as land use changes and species characteristics, effectively “select” a risk model for each species-location, but weather shocks contribute to the actual category transitions (i.e., from endangered to extinct-in-the-wild).
> 
> METHODS:
> Use an ordered logit model to simultaneously fit weather shocks impacts and risk models: For a linear model, we have yitOrderedLogit(βk(i,t)Wit+αi+δt,{θc }) where yit is a categorical outcome (either Red List status or population trend) for a species i in year t. βk(i,t) is the risk coefficient for that species, which depends on species characteristics and land use change, under a hierarchical relationship (not shown). Wit could include temperatures, heat waves, and droughts. {θc } is the central value for each of the ordered categories (e.g., {θVulnerable, θEndangered, θExtinct}).
> Construct improved willingness-to-pay (WTP) valuations by species, drawing on benefit transfer approaches: We will analyse meta-analysis datasets like Johnston et al. (2006) and Nobel et al. (2020) to generalise WTP values across species and regions. To rationalise this data, we build on Noack et al. (2025), by calibrating a level of "substitutability" between species that simultaneously predicts the WTP results for species abundance, species richness, and individual species/habitats.
> Incorporate these dynamic risks into a spatial optimization framework, accounting for robust decision-making: Here, we build a model to identify areas for biodiversity conservation, accounting for future evolution of land use change (multiple scenarios, building on Taylor et al. 2021) and uncertainty in transitions and WTP estimates. This model will use linear optimisation techniques that maximise total conservation benefit, drawing upon Polasky et al. (2008) and Rising (2020).
